The legal texts come from the Government of Niger's portal (www.gouv.ne), Textes fondamentaux, retrieved
29 October 2026:

  Constitution de la VIIème République (décret n°2010-754/PCSRD du 25 novembre 2010), with the modifications
  to the loi n°2018-44 du 22 juin 2018.

The portal publishes no licence for these texts. They are the public law of the Republic of Niger, reproduced with
the attribution above; their copyright status is not confirmed here.

The Code de procédure pénale (loi n° 61-33 du 14 août 1961, as amended by lois n° 2003-026 and 2004-21) is copied
from the PDF held by UNODC SHERLOC
(https://sherloc.unodc.org/cld/uploads/res/document/ner/1961/code_de_procedure_penale_html/Niger_CODE_DE_PROCEDURE_PENALE.pdf),
retrieved on 30 October 2026. The PDF states no licence; it is the text of a law of the Republic of Niger.

The Code du travail (loi n° 2012-45 du 25 septembre 2012) is copied from a PDF published by Droit-Afrique
(http://www.droit-afrique.com/upload/doc/niger/Niger-Code-2012-du-travail.pdf), as archived by the Internet Archive
on 15 June 2016, retrieved on 30 October 2026. Credit for the bytes: Droit-Afrique. Niger is a member of the African
Intellectual Property Organization (OAPI), and Article 6(i) of Annex VII of the Bangui Agreement excludes "les textes
officiels de nature législative, administrative ou judiciaire" from copyright protection.

Changes made by Lexifina: the PDF text layer was converted to one Markdown file per provision, one paragraph or item
per line; page numbers were dropped; spacing, quotes and dashes were normalised; amendment notes in article headings
were left out. The wording of the law was not changed. The Government of Niger does not endorse this repository.

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the tools) are
dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode
